    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on my thesis regarding the kinetics of the Maillard reaction in protein-rich foods. Could you explain the specific role that amino acids and reducing sugars play in the initial stages of the reaction, and specifically how variations in pH levels influence the rate of non-enzymatic browning?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the initial stages of the Maillard reaction, reducing sugars act as nucleophiles that react with the amino group of amino acids or peptides to form a Schiff base. This intermediate rapidly rearranges into a more stable Amadori product, driving the early progression of non-enzymatic browning. Variations in pH significantly influence the reaction rate because the availability of nucleophilic amino amines increases as pH rises, thereby accelerating the reaction until extreme alkalinity stabilizes the Schiff base product. Consequently, higher pH environments generally promote faster browning compared to acidic conditions, making pH a critical control parameter in food processing and storage.",
      "source": "target",
