    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. To go a bit deeper into the kinetics, how do different types of amino acids—specifically those with different R-groups like lysine versus those that are more hydrophobic—alter the complexity of the final melanoidin compounds? Also, does the specific type of reducing sugar (e.g., glucose vs. fructose) fundamentally change the pathway or just the speed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Different amino acids with polar side chains, like lysine, often lead to more heterogeneous melanoidin structures compared to those with hydrophobic R-groups that may favor specific aggregation pathways. While glucose, fructose, and sorbose are chemically isomers, fructose typically reacts more rapidly to form melanoidins faster, yet it generally yields fewer and more easily oxidized final products than glucose or maltose. The final pathway remains fundamentally the same—consisting of the Strecker degradation and advanced glycation end-products—but the kinetics are distinctly shaped by the sugar's volatility and stability.",
      "source": "target",
